  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>If possible, provide access to devices that can run Desmos or other graphing technology.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which of these scatter plots shows data that would best be modeled with a linear function? Be prepared to show
    your reasoning. </item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-justify-content-between">
  <para>Graph A <figure><media alt="Scatterplot."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a2cffe2402ce74466cff9ac61ce61ebe2133f218"/></media></figure></para>
       <para>Graph B<figure><media alt="Scatter plot."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/de0a019009ffffea916b212daa4e99a1b8c71862"/></media></figure></para>
 </div>
<list class="os-raise-noindent&gt;" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Graph B. Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. The points in scatter plot B seem to follow the shape of a line of best fit. The points in scatter plot in A are very spread out.</para>
  <item>Which of the following lines is most likely the line of best fit for the data provided?</item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-justify-content-between">
  <list>
    <item>\(y=-0.51x+225.12\)</item>
    <item>\(y=0.34x−34.05\)</item>
    <item>\(y=0.13x−0.19\)</item>
    <item>\(y=0.98x−21.13\)</item>
  </list>
  <figure><media alt="A scatterplot."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8dafb6a04f31f990137ee877aac9e178b67e8db3"/></media></figure></div>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"> number
        of trees in a forest </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"> tons
        of oxygen produced by the forest </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry> 148 </entry>
      <entry> 16.43 </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> 175 </entry>
      <entry> 25.64 </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> 190 </entry>
      <entry> 23.28 </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> 200 </entry>
      <entry> 29.2 </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> 202 </entry>
      <entry> 21.41 </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> 425 </entry>
      <entry> 60.56 </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> 505 </entry>
      <entry> 50.75 </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> 528 </entry>
      <entry> 74.45 </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> 562 </entry>
      <entry> 62.66 </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> 585 </entry>
      <entry> 84.24 </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis>\(y=0.13x-0.19\)</para>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>Points to emphasize</title>
<para>Unit 3 Project provides an additional opportunity to work with fitted lines.</para>
</section>
